\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{Notation for foundations}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The exterior derivative on cochains is #{\extd}, and it is also
#{\covwedge}. The counting adjoint is #{\extdag}.}

\p{A square is #{\covsq}. The positive operator #{\Delta} is the
counterpart #{-\covsq}. The weighted operator is
#{L=-\extdag W\extd}, so a formula that writes #{\extdag W\extd}
is writing #{-L}.}

\p{The boundary of a chain is #{\partial}. A difference along a
direction #{a} is #{a\cdot\cov}. The function #{\delta_x} is given
by #{\delta_x(y)=\delta_{xy}}.}

\p{An adjoint is #{A^{\dagger}} (\ref{fdip-001G}, \ref{fdip-00QN}).}

\p{A pullback #{a^*} and a dual module #{V^*} keep the star, and the
identity map is #{\bbI} (\ref{fdip-00AQ}, \ref{fdip-00QO}).}

\p{The undergraduate curl stays #{\operatorname{curl}}, and the Hodge
dual is right multiplication by #{\psI^{-1}}
(\ref{fdip-00AA}, \ref{fdip-00QQ}).}

\p{A projection is #{\pr}, and an inclusion is #{\iota}
(\ref{fdip-001I}, \ref{fdip-00QT}).}

\p{The image of an operator is #{\im}
(\ref{fdip-00K6}, \ref{fdip-00QV}).}

\p{The dimension of a grid is #{n}, and an edge or basis element is
#{e} (\ref{fdip-00NZ}, \ref{fdip-00QS}).}

\p{The continuum commutator is #{\bigl[\tfrac{d}{dx},x\bigr]=1}
(\ref{fdip-00AP}, \ref{fdip-00RU}).}

\p{Operators and distinguished elements such as #{\extd} and #{\bbI}
are chosen so they are visually distinct and semantically consistent
throughout. Where Hestenes's geometric algebra and geometric calculus
(\citek{hestenes2012clifford}) have a counterpart, their notation is
used to keep related operations unified under one algebraic grammar:
here the discrete exterior derivative #{\extd} is also written
#{\covwedge} (the grade-raising part of the single derivative
#{\cov}, matching exterior differentiation on forms while its
grade-lowering counterpart #{\covdot} enters with Clifford algebras
in Part V). Elsewhere, mathematical-physics notation is the default
fallback, as for cochains, pairings, and differences. A familiar
symbol is reused only when its meaning here agrees with or extends
the familiar one, and the Remark after its first definition gives
the familiar form.}
